.\" bmb_suite(1). make turns bmb_suite.1.in into bmb_suite.1, then into the
.\" site's bmb_suite.html; edit the .in. man/test_man_*.sh check it
.\" against the programs: see doc/dev/documentation.md.
.TH BMB_SUITE 1 2026-10-29 "blas-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation, no justification: both hurt in a terminal.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
bmb_suite \- run the blas\-microbenchmark suite into one directory of results
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmb_suite
.RB [ \-\-level
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-precision
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-routine
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR profile ]
.RB [ \-\-blas
.IR name = suite ]...
.RB [ \-\-blas
.BI flexiblas: name ,...]
.RB [ \-n ]
.B \-o
.I dir
.RB [ \-\-
.IR "benchmark option" ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bmb_suite
runs the benchmarks of
.BR blas\-microbenchmark (1),
every one or a selection, one after the other, and writes their results
into one directory, which
.BR bmb_report (1)
takes whole:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_suite \-o results \-\-profile quick
bmb_report results > report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
The benchmarks it runs are those installed with it, in
.IR @pkglibexecdir@ ;
run from a build tree
.RI ( build/src/suite/bmb_suite ),
those of that tree.
A routine missing from an older install is left out, and said; one
asked for by
.B \-\-routine
is refused.
.SS The results
Each routine writes
.IR dir / routine .json,
and what the benchmark printed goes to
.IR dir / routine .log.
.I dir
is created, and refused when it already holds files: the results of two
runs would mix in the report.
A line on standard error says which routine runs, and how many are left.
.PP
A benchmark that fails, a result wrong under
.B \-\-verify
or an option it refuses, is said with the end of its log, and the others
still run; one that skips (exit 77, a batch mode the library does not
have) is said too.
.SS Sizes and profiles
Without
.BR \-\-profile ,
each benchmark runs at its level's default size.
A profile gives the sizes instead: a line a level
.RB ( level1 ,
.BR level2 ,
.BR level3 )
or a routine, then the options it is run with; a routine's line wins
over its level's.
A
.B #
starts a comment.
Two are shipped.
.B quick
measures a few sizes a level, about a minute on one core of a laptop:
.PP
.RS
.EX
level1 \-v 1000,100000,10000000
level2 \-m 200,2000
level3 \-m 200,1000
sgbmv \-m 200,2000 \-M 32
dgbmv \-m 200,2000 \-M 32
stbsv \-m 200,2000 \-M 32
dtbsv \-m 200,2000 \-M 32
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B full
doubles the size from the first-level cache to memory at levels 1 and 2,
and up to 2048 at level 3, several minutes on the same core:
.PP
.RS
.EX
level1 \-v 256:16777216
level2 \-m 32:4096
level3 \-m 32:2048
sgbmv \-m 32:4096 \-M 32
dgbmv \-m 32:4096 \-M 32
stbsv \-m 32:4096 \-M 32
dtbsv \-m 32:4096 \-M 32
.EE
.RE
.PP
The band routines get a band of 32 elements: by default, theirs is as
wide as the matrix.
.SS Several libraries
The benchmarks measure the library they were built against: a
blas\-microbenchmark a library.
.B \-\-blas
.IR name = suite
runs the one in
.IR suite ,
an install prefix (where
.I libexec/blas\-microbenchmark
is), a build tree (where
.I src/c
is) or the directory of its levels, into
.IR dir / name .
It can be given once a library; then the suite
.B bmb_suite
is installed with is not run, unless given too.
The selection, the profile and the options apply to each, and
.BR bmb_report (1)
takes
.I dir
whole, a series a library.
.PP
Each benchmark is run with
.B \-\-label
.IR name ,
which tells apart two builds of one library (two versions, two
compilers) in the report; a name with the library's version in it
.RB ( openblas\-0.3.26 )
lets the report draw the versions' history, so
.B \-l
is refused among the options passed on.
The backend each suite was built for is read from its benchmarks'
.B \-\-version
before anything runs, and each result must record it: one recording
another fails, rather than becoming a series under the wrong name.
A name is letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
each given once.
.PP
With Spack, a blas\-microbenchmark a library:
.PP
.RS
.EX
spack install blas\-microbenchmark ^openblas
spack install blas\-microbenchmark ^blis
spack install blas\-microbenchmark ^intel\-oneapi\-mkl
O=$(spack location \-i blas\-microbenchmark ^openblas)
B=$(spack location \-i blas\-microbenchmark ^blis)
M=$(spack location \-i blas\-microbenchmark ^intel\-oneapi\-mkl)
bmb_suite \-\-blas openblas=$O \-\-blas blis=$B \-\-blas mkl=$M \-\-profile quick \-o results
bmb_report results > report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
or the three in a Spack environment, a
.I spack.yaml
of its own directory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
spack:
  specs:
  \- blas\-microbenchmark ^openblas
  \- blas\-microbenchmark ^blis
  \- blas\-microbenchmark ^intel\-oneapi\-mkl
  concretizer:
    unify: false
.EE
.RE
.PP
which
.B "spack \-e . install"
builds, and
.B "spack \-e . location \-i"
then finds, as above.
.PP
A build against FlexiBLAS loads the library
.B FLEXIBLAS
names when a benchmark starts, and records it
.RB ( flexiblas/openblas\-openmp ).
.B \-\-blas
.BI flexiblas: name ,...
runs the suite
.B bmb_suite
is installed with, which must be built against FlexiBLAS, once a
library, with
.B FLEXIBLAS
set to its name, into
.IR dir / name :
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_suite \-\-blas flexiblas:OPENBLAS\-OPENMP,BLIS\-OPENMP \-\-profile quick \-o results
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B "flexiblas list"
gives the names.
For a name it does not know, FlexiBLAS loads its default library, so
each name is checked before anything runs: the benchmarks'
.B \-\-version
must record it.
Both forms of
.B \-\-blas
can be given together.
What it costs: FlexiBLAS must be installed with its libraries (Fedora
packages it, and Spack builds it; Debian and Ubuntu do not), and its
indirection, a call through a pointer, shows on the smallest level 1
calls.
.SS Options for every benchmark
The words after
.B \-\-
go to every benchmark, after the profile's:
.B "\-t 1,8"
for the thread counts,
.BR "\-\-cache cold" ,
.BR "\-i 50" .
.B bmb_suite
gives each
.B "\-f json \-o"
itself, last.
It refuses
.B \-o
and
.B \-f
there, and
.BR \-\-peak\-gflops ,
whose value is in the routine's precision and would be wrong for half
of them: give the peak to
.BR bmb_report (1)
instead.
An option a routine has no use for is ignored with a warning in its
log; one it refuses
.RB ( \-\-trans
on
.BR ddot )
makes it fail.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-o, \-\-output " dir
Where the results go: created, and refused when it already holds files.
.TP
.BI "\-\-level " list
Only the routines of these levels, a list such as
.BR 1,2 .
.TP
.BI "\-\-precision " list
Only the routines of these precisions, of
.BR s ,
.BR d ,
.B c
and
.BR z ,
a list such as
.BR d,z .
.TP
.BI "\-\-routine " list
Only these routines, a list such as
.BR dgemm,ddot .
With
.B \-\-level
or
.BR \-\-precision ,
those of them that are also of the levels and precisions given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " profile
The sizes:
.BR quick ,
.B full
or a profile file (see
.BR "Sizes and profiles" ).
Default: each level's default size.
.TP
.BI "\-\-blas " name = suite
Run the blas\-microbenchmark of
.IR suite ,
an install prefix or a build tree, into
.IR dir / name ;
once a library (see
.BR "Several libraries" ).
Default: the suite
.B bmb_suite
is installed with, into
.IR dir .
.TP
.BI "\-\-blas flexiblas:" name ,...
The same for each library the FlexiBLAS that the suite
.B bmb_suite
is installed with is built against loads, with
.B FLEXIBLAS
set to its name.
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-dry\-run"
Print the commands, one a line, and run nothing.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Show a summary of the usage.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Show the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every benchmark ran or skipped;
1 when one failed, or wrote a result of another backend than its
suite's, after the others ran;
2 for a usage error, said before anything runs.
.SH EXAMPLES
The quick profile on 1 and 8 threads, then the report:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_suite \-\-profile quick \-o results \-\- \-t 1,8
bmb_report results > report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Level 3 in double precision, at the default size, cold:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_suite \-\-level 3 \-\-precision d,z \-o level3 \-\- \-\-cache cold
.EE
.RE
.PP
What a profile would run, without running it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_suite \-n \-\-profile full \-\-routine dgemm \-o results
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR blas\-microbenchmark (1),
.BR bmb_report (1)
.PP
The guides \(em installing, comparing libraries, on an HPC cluster,
ReFrame \(em and this manual online:
https://mathieucaissa.github.io/blas\-microbenchmark/
